AB  - Favorable neighborhood environments are critical determinants of population respiratory health. However, existing studies have been constrained by linear model assumptions, multicollinearity, and contextual disparities in urban configurations, limiting their ability to provide actionable insights for precision health-oriented urban planning. This study bridges these gaps by examining the old urban district of Bengbu City, integrating respiratory disease mortality (RDM) data (2016-2020) with multi-source geospatial datasets. We employ the eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) machine learning model with Partial Dependence Plots (PDPs) that incorporate confidence intervals to decode nonlinear environment-health associations and identify critical thresholds. The results indicate that 1) RDM exhibits strong spatial clustering, concentrated in densely populated and highly built-up neighborhoods, forming a "two-core, multiple dispersed centers" pattern; 2) blue-green space is the dominant dimension influencing RDM (35.64 % contribution), with distance to the nearest park, intersection density, and tobacco and alcohol facility density identified as key variables; and 3) nearly all neighborhood environmental factors exhibit complex nonlinear associations with RDM, with several critical thresholds identified. Specifically, optimal respiratory outcomes are observed when tobacco and alcohol facility density reaches 0.38 pcs/ha, intersection density is 0.13 pcs/ha, building density is 30 %, and the distance to the nearest park is within 450 meters. These insights provide robust empirical evidence for policymaking and spatial governance, supporting SDG 3 and SDG 11.
