AB  - Background: General practitioners (GPs) play a central role in the detection and management of alcohol use disorders (AUDs). In this cross-sectional study, we aimed to investigate the existing skills, needs and demands of German GPs and to identify potential targets that lead to an improvement of care.
   Methods: Half of the total population of the GPs in the region South-West Germany (n = 1232) were sent a questionnaire regarding the prevalence of AUDs, the last patient treated, perceived role, potential for improvement and need for further education.
   Results: A total of 299 questionnaires were sent back and could be included in the analysis (24.3% response rate). The GPs perceived themselves as responsible for the detection and treatment of AUDs in various modalities. In multivariate analysis, higher perceived responsibility resulted not only in a higher demand for continuing education and training, but also a higher perceived knowledge and skills.
   Conclusion: Measures to improve the quality of care of AUDs can be aimed at different targets in the system: in addition to supporting increased role legitimacy, solid basis for the development of skills ( role adequacy) should be fostered. Preferred contents of education are: screening strategies, brief interventional techniques, relapse management and involvement strategies for relatives.
